# Designer workflow in Cursor

This plugin is for **designers**, not deep engineering codegen. Default to inspect, summarize, and review.

## Default behavior

1. **Inspect before implement** — Run inspect/handoff before any code generation.
2. **Ask the platform** — React Native, SwiftUI, or Android if implementation is requested.
3. **One screen at a time** — Hand off frame-by-frame, not entire files unless asked.
4. **Preserve design intent** — Call out ambiguities instead of guessing engineering behavior.

## When to use each capability

| Designer intent | Use |
| --- | --- |
| "What's in this frame?" / specs for dev | `inspect-design` command or `figma-inspect-handoff` skill |
| "Summarize our workshop board" | `figjam-summary` command |
| "Rough RN/SwiftUI for this screen" | `figma-design-to-code` skill (mobile, single screen) |
| "Map component to code" (future) | `figma-code-connect` skill (stub) |

## Review checklist (before marking handoff ready)

- [ ] Frame URL includes `node-id`
- [ ] All interactive states documented (default, pressed, disabled, error)
- [ ] Typography and colors captured with units
- [ ] Safe areas and scroll behavior described
- [ ] Assets/export notes listed (icons, illustrations)
- [ ] Platform-specific variants noted (iOS vs Android)
- [ ] Open questions assigned (design vs engineering)

## What not to do in MVP

- Do not sync or export design tokens (no token path configured).
- Do not scaffold full apps, navigation stacks, or CI pipelines unless explicitly requested.
- Do not replace Figma — Cursor augments handoff and review.

## Example prompts (designer-friendly)

- "Inspect this mobile login screen for handoff: [Figma URL]"
- "Summarize decisions from this FigJam retro: [FigJam URL]"
- "Review this checkout frame for accessibility and touch targets: [Figma URL]"
- "Suggest SwiftUI structure for this profile header only: [Figma URL]"
